{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-sector-rotation-and-business-cycles",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 4: Does Sector Rotation Actually Work in India?",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Case Study: The 2020 to 2024 Rotation From Pharma and IT to PSU Banks and Capital Goods",
          "slug": "case-study-the-2020-to-2024-rotation-from-pharma-and-it-to-psu-banks-and-capital-goods",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "c4l2k001",
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "Big rotations rarely announce themselves. They show up first in relative performance, then in company fundamentals, and last of all in the headlines, by which time much of the move has already happened."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l2k002",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l2k002s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Lesson 4.1 tested the rotation clock year by year. This lesson follows one complete rotation from start to finish: the shift in Indian market leadership from pandemic winners, pharma and IT, to the sectors of the government-led capex and banking upcycle, PSU banks and public sector capital goods, energy, and defence companies. Every return figure comes from NSE index closing values.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l2k003",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l2k003s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The Rotation in One Chart",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l2k004",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l2k004md2",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A sector index divided by a benchmark index such as the Nifty 50, rebased to 100 at a starting date. A rising line means the sector is outperforming the benchmark; a falling line means it is underperforming, regardless of whether prices are rising or falling."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l2k004s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The chart below shows ",
